    <node><name>0</name><description>Value</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>ta</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>1.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-1-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>2</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-1-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>1</name><description>Form: fully expanded</description><answertest>EqualComAssRules</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>ta</tans><testoptions>[oneMul]</testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>1.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-2-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-2-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your answer has the right value, but it is not fully expanded. Multiply out every bracket and collect like terms, so that there is one term for each power of \(x\).</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>2</name><description>Powered each term</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>fresh</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-3-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>You raised each term to the power \({@n@}\) on its own, but \({@brs@}\) is not \({@fresh@}\). It means \({@n@}\) brackets multiplied together, so there are \({@np1@}\) terms.</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>3</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-3-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>3</name><description>Did not power p</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>notpow</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-4-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>You wrote \(({@px@})^{k}\) as \({@p@}x^{k}\). The whole term \({@px@}\) is raised to each power: \(({@px@})^{@n@} = {@pnxn@}\), not \({@pxn@}\), and \(({@px@})^2 = {@p2x2@}\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>4</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-4-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>4</name><description>Sign pattern wrong</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>flip</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-5-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[[[if test="is(q<0)"]]<p>All your terms have the same sign, but the bracket contains a minus sign: the terms with an odd power of \({@q@}\) are negative, so the signs alternate.</p>[[else]]<p>Your signs alternate, but \({@brs@}\) has no minus sign, so every term is positive.</p>[[/if]]]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-5-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Use the coefficients {@crow@} and put \(a = {@px@}\) and \(b = {@q@}\) into each term, with brackets: coefficient \(\times\) \(({@px@})^{\text{power}} \times ({@q@})^{\text{power}}\). Check: \(x = 1\) should give \({@chk@}\).</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
